<title>UK: UK retail prices rise yr/yr 2.5 pct in Dec.</title>
<headline>UK retail prices rise yr/yr 2.5 pct in Dec.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-01-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Office for National Statistics released the following retail price indices for December:</p>
<p>    RETAIL PRICES INDEX	   DEC	  NOV	 DEC 95</p>
<p>    Mthly RPI pct change	  0.3	  0.1	 0.6</p>
<p>    Yr-on-yr RPI pct change     2.5	  2.7	 3.2</p>
<p>    RPI Index (base Jan 1987)   154.4	153.9     150.7</p>
<p>    Mth change minus mortgages  0.3	  0.1	 0.5</p>
<p>    Yr-on-yr minus mortgages    3.1	  3.3	 3.0</p>
<p>    Index minus mortgages	 154.2	153.7     149.6</p>
<p>    Mth pct change ex MIPs, tax -0.1	  0.1	 0.2</p>
<p>    Yr-on-yr change ex MIPs,tax  2.7	 3.0	 2.5</p>
<p>    Index ex MIPS, indirect tax 149.5	149.7     145.6</p>
<p>COMPONENT DETAILS OF THE ALL-ITEMS INDEX (PCT CHANGES) -</p>
<p>				    DEC     YR/YR	NOV     YR/YR</p>
<p>    Food			  0.1     0.8	 -0.4	1.5</p>
<p>    seasonal food	     2.0    -8.1	 -0.6     -6.2</p>
<p>    non-seasonal food	-0.2     2.3	 -0.3	2.8</p>
<p>  Catering			0.3     4.0	  0.2	4.0</p>
<p>  Alcoholic drink	    -0.4     3.3	 -0.2	2.9</p>
<p>  Tobacco			 2.0     6.4	 -0.2	6.9</p>
<p>  Housing			 0.1     1.7	  0.2	1.6</p>
<p>  Fuel and light	     -0.1    -0.7	 -0.5     -0.4</p>
<p>  Household goods	     1.0     2.3	  1.O	2.5</p>
<p>  Household services	  0.1     1.1	  UNCH     0.9</p>
<p>  Clothing and footwear    -0.2     0.2	  1.1	0.6</p>
<p>  Personal goods &amp; services 0.4     3.8	  0.2	3.5</p>
<p>  Motoring expenditure	0.9     5.6	 -0.4	6.8</p>
<p>  Fares &amp; travel costs	unch    3.6	 -0.2	3.5</p>
<p>  Leisure goods		-0.1     1.1	  0.6	1.7</p>
<p>  Leisure services	    0.5     3.9	  0.2	3.6</p>
<p>NOTE - Consensus forecast was RPI up 0.5 pct MM,+2.6 pct YY, RPIX +3.1 pct YY, RPIY +3.2 pct YY.  </p>
<p>(MIPS=mortgage interest payments)</p>
<p>The ONS said fall in the 12 month rate of RPI was largely due to motoring costs and food prices, though there was also a smaller downward effect from leisure goods' prices. Upward effects on the 12-month rate came from housing costs and alcoholic drinks' prices.</p>
</text>
